< header class="page"
  = {{ res.data.title }}
  //{{ res.data.subtitle }}//

A line starting with ``<`` opens an element, and everything indented two spaces
past it is its content. The closing tag is never written: the end of the
indent is the end of the element. This is a rule of the grammar, not a rewrite
over the top of one — the parser opens a node and closes it, so an element
that runs off the end of the file ends there and nowhere else.

< div class="callout"
  == Every block rule works at every depth

  The indentation is structure, so a heading, a list, a table or a fence all
  read the same in here as they do at the margin:

  - !!bold!! and ``code`` work
  - so do [[ links | https://example.com ]]

  | Marker   | Element |
  | :------- | :------ |
  | two bangs | strong |
This paragraph is outside the callout: the indent ended, so the element did.

== Nesting, and generated content

Elements nest — each level is two more columns — and because a ``%`` line is
lifted out before any column is counted, code can sit anywhere it reads best
without moving the markup it encloses.

< section class="team"
  < div class="roster"
    % for (const m of res.data.members) {
    - !!{{ m.name }}!! — {{ m.role }}{{ m.lead ? ' //(lead)//' : '' }}
    % }

  < div class="count"
    {{ res.data.members.length }} people, {{ res.data.members.filter((m) => m.lead).length }} of them leads.

== Nesting another document

``$.render`` gives back a whole document's finished tree, and ``\{{ }}`` is where
it goes. There is no indent to pass: the parser knows which element is open
where the token landed, so the returned tree simply becomes its child.

< section class="roster-cards"
  % for (const m of res.data.members) {
  < article class="card"
    {{ $.render({ template: 'card' }, m) }}
  % }

That is also why one document cannot damage another. Each is parsed at its own
boundary and comes back as a node, so an element left open in the card reaches
the end of the card and stops. There is no such thing as an unclosed element
in a tree.

== Element kinds

Void elements stand alone; they never get a closing tag:

<hr class="rule"

<img src="team.png" alt="The team"

Raw-text elements keep their body exactly as written — de-indented, but never
read as markup:

< pre class="ascii"
     _____
    /     \    //not// emphasis
   |  o o  |   and    the    spacing    survives
    \_____/

< style
  .callout { border-left: 3px solid #ccc; padding-left: 1rem }

== What an opener is not

An opener shown inside a fence is sample text, not structure:

```mdy
< div class="example"
  This block is displayed, not expanded.
```
